  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Periods of showers/scattered thunderstorms \nthrough Saturday will bring localized flash flooding risk across\ncentral Pennsylvania. Strong-to-severe storms also possible \nthis afternoon/evening.\n\nActivity on Thursday was largely driven by the terrain in\ncombination with a very moist airmass. Overall a similar\nsituation today and Saturday. Location of the more active\nweather on Saturday may be a bit further to the east and south. \nThat is still a ways out.\n\nFor the overnight hours into Friday morning, the main concern\naside from an isolated shower will be the potential for fog/low\nstratus development with mainly clear skies and calm winds.\n\nMore information below. \n\nThis concern will be highest in areas that received heavy \nrainfall across southern Pennsylvania on Thursday. Updated fog \nforecast does outline this potential with valley fog expected \nacross the northern half of central Pennsylvania. Any fog \nformation likely mixes out quickly after sunrise and is out by \n9AM EDT.\n\nFirst glance at the first couple of hours of 00Z 8/7 HREF \nensemble guidance outlines an uptick in shower activity across\nnorthern Ohio overnight compared to previous runs. Thus, have\nincreased PoPs slightly earlier across NW PA closer to sunrise\ncompared to previous forecast. Increasing humidity throughout\nthe day with ample instability (~1000-2000 J/kg across N/W PA \nand 2000+ J/kg across S/E PA) will likely allow for slightly \nmore widespread coverage with more height falls observed in \nrecent model guidance compared to this afternoon and evening. \nIn terms of a severe threat, expect scattered thunderstorms to \ndevelop with potentially a slight uptick in coverage compared to\nthis afternoon. Isolated instances of strong-to-damaging winds \nappear to be the main hazard with these, mainly in the form of \nwet microbursts given the instability, moisture, and lift \n(trough approaching the area) in play.\n\nThe overly moist air mass, with PWATs exceeding the 90th\npercentile across all of central Pennsylvania, will brings\nanother round of flash flooding concerns on Friday and Saturday,\nmainly during the afternoon/evening hours. This flooding threat\ncould be minimized by more progressive storm motions; however,\ngiven the amount of moisture, any shower/storm will likely bring\nefficient rainfall to the region and any location (especially \nurban and poor drainage locations) that receive multiple rounds \nof heavy rainfall could experience localized flash flooding. \nRecent HREF PMMs does paint a corridor 2-3\" rainfall amounts \nalong the Pennsylvania Turnpike Friday afternoon/evening, which \ntracks with the ample moisture available. Do want to highlight \nthat while this signal exists, location of this corridor remains\nthe main point of uncertainty at this time so remain weather \naware and have multiple ways to receive warnings tomorrow \nafternoon/evening. Heavy rain signal on Saturday is more muted; \nhowever, if this heavier corridor of rain plays out as \npredicted, some locations could need very little rainfall for \nadditional flooding concerns on Saturday. \n\n   ------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Very warm temperatures and humid conditions will\nlead to heat index values approaching 100 degrees across \nportions of the Lower Susquehanna Valley today.\n\nCurrent grids only show a few grid points getting close. \nAlso temperatures being push back down by showers and \nstorms, temperatures may not stay up in that range for\nvery long anyway. \n\nMore information below.\n\nIncreasing heat and humidity throughout the afternoon hours \nahead of the aforementioned showers/storms will lead to another \nhot day across portions of the Lower Susquehanna Valley and \nsurrounding locations. Recent model guidance does indicate a \nslight decrease in temperatures tomorrow with increasing cloud \ncover during the late morning/early afternoon hours across the \nregion which could limit peak heating slightly. That being said,\nmoisture will be plentiful with dew points expected to reach \ninto the mid-70s during the afternoon hours ahead of forecast \nprecipitation. Recent HREF guidance of heat index values \nexceeding 100F appear to be more confined to the south and east \nof Lancaster County. Highest probabilities max out around \n~30-40% for far southern Lancaster County. For reference, \nsimilar guidance was highlight probabilities closer to ~60-70% \nfor similar locations this afternoon/evening. Given the \nuncertainty, have decided to let the upcoming midnight shift \nhandle heat headlines for Friday to get another run of HREF/NBM \nmodel guidance to assist in this decision. Whether or not a heat\nheadline gets posted, hot and humid conditions are expected for\nthe Lower Susquehanna Valley tomorrow before a slight decrease \nin heat/humidity for the upcoming weekend.",
